					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/we-don-t-know-it-exists</guid>
				<title>The Door</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/we-don-t-know-it-exists</link>
				<description>

&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: right;&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The boundless, dark nature of what&#039;s beyond that door shall never be explored. If you walk beyond the border, expect to be never seen again. We did not know what horrors this door contained just fifteen meters under the wildlands of the African Sahara, but once people started disappearing we began to understand. Everytime I stared at it, fear would flow through my veins. The door was not normal. It&#039;s green paint appearing to leak, turn red, or even move towards me- but with a blink it was gone. At first it was thought to be psychological in nature, nothing that could hurt me or my team.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That was not true.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One night my good friend, the digger of the team, moved closer to the door. Every step he took trembled through his bones. When he stopped, right next to the green door, he began to bleed. From his ears and mouth, every opening in his body began to gush out; but he did not respond. He simply stood there, the door unmoving, bleeding out. Once he seemed pale beyond comprehension, he simply opened it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He opened the door.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tentacles of some kind moved to his feet and arms and dragged him inside. The door slammed shut and we just stood there, astonished at the horrors of this door. That day we dug for hours to bury its horrors from whence it came, waiting for backup to assist, but the radios were out. No signal in or out; a field of nothing around us. When we noticed it, we felt it. Every day we were stranded there goosebumps would appear and disappear just as quick. Steps behind you that aren&#039;t there, the feeling that you&#039;re being watched, or that something is behind you.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We needed help.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Second to go was our field medic. He punched the sand that we buried the door in out of anger and he fell inside. The sand vanished around him and devoured him whole. Before he could shout or yell he was already inside, the sand sealing the opening the moment his feet came to an end in the dark abyss below. For a few days we swore we could hear his disconcerting screams. Yelling, shouting to us for help. Yet when we mentioned it to each-other it was never at the same time. A weird hum filled the hut we stayed in one day, and the shouting stopped. But we still felt the door&#039;s influence. In fact we felt it grow ever since it was opened. We were scared of our own future; hoping not to become victim of this vicious curse.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A curse it was.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Almost magical we thought. Realistically impossible. Yet in our dreams the nightmares persisted. The door took over what silence we had left. Every day the focus was on it. The door. That door. The green devil&#039;s door, leading to the abyss or the deepest level of hell. We were so sure of it, would we be able to run, we would have. But three-hundred kilometers away from any civilization, and without the motor vehicle functioning due to something we haven&#039;t understood yet, left us stranded. We thought for just a moment that maybe the door broke the vehicle, but at this point it did not matter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It did not matter to the door.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When I awoke one morning to inspect our surroundings I noticed a leg in the sand dune, attached to what I feared was the body of our survivalist. When I went to look at it, the sand around it vibrated with a massive power and an underground hum that instilled primal, animal fear in me. I ran, ran away so far and so quick I did not see the leg disappear, only what remained of it. Bloody sand burrowing its way to the door.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bloody sand was coming towards me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I stood there, frozen in time. but when the sand, bloodied by whoever was eaten that day, came closer inch by inch towards me, the last member of the team, I moved only a little. Something grabbed me by the heels and I could not budge. The hum appeared again. A deep tremble, vibrations like the thump of a drum. Every second it would beat. I got loose by fiddling with the sand below me, only inches before certain death. I ran. Ran into whatever the Sahara had in for me. I knew I would die, and now, writing this, I notice the blood dripping from the roof of the sandstone cave I hid in. I don&#039;t have much time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My family, I&#039;m sorry. So sorry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;by &lt;span class=&quot;printuser avatarhover&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/archie-inkel&quot;  &gt;&lt;img class=&quot;small&quot; src=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5235771&amp;amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;amp;timestamp=1607008302&quot; alt=&quot;Archie Inkel&quot; style=&quot;background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5235771)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/archie-inkel&quot;  &gt;Archie Inkel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
